		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>A professional end of tenancy clean usually takes around three to five hours for a studio or one-bedroom flat, and roughly a full working day for a three-bedroom house, with a team sized to the job. If you're doing it yourself, allow most of a day for a small flat and two days or more for a family home.</p>
<p>Size is only half the story. The state of the oven, the amount of limescale in the bathroom and whether the carpets need cleaning can all add hours. Here's a realistic guide for properties in Hull and East Yorkshire, and how to plan your move-out around it.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Professional teams usually finish a small flat in around half a day and a three-bed house in around a day.</li><li>Condition matters as much as size: heavy grease, limescale, pet hair and mould all add time.</li><li>Doing it yourself takes much longer, so set aside a clear day or two once your belongings are out.</li><li>Carpets need time to dry after cleaning, so don’t leave the clean until the morning of check-out.</li></ul></div>
<h2>End of tenancy cleaning times by property size</h2>
<p>The table below is a rough guide to how long an end of tenancy clean takes in an empty property in average condition. Larger homes are normally cleaned by several cleaners working together, which is why time on site doesn't rise in step with the number of rooms.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Property</th><th>Professional team (rough guide)</th><th>Doing it yourself (rough guide)</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Studio or one-bed flat</td><td>Around 3 to 5 hours</td><td>Most of a day</td></tr>
<tr><td>Two-bed flat or terrace</td><td>Around 4 to 7 hours</td><td>A long day, often running into a second</td></tr>
<tr><td>Three-bed house</td><td>Around 6 to 9 hours</td><td>Two days or more</td></tr>
<tr><td>Four-bed house or shared house</td><td>A full day, usually with a larger team</td><td>Several days</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>These are estimates for a standard clean. Carpet cleaning, extra appliances and heavily soiled ovens come on top. When we quote for <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy cleaning</a>, we ask about the property first so we can send the right size of team and give you a realistic timescale.</p>
<h2>Which parts of an end of tenancy clean take longest?</h2>
<h3>The oven</h3>
<p>A lightly used oven is fairly quick, but one with years of carbon on the door glass and racks can take a long time on its own. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:how-long-does-oven-cleaning-take}}">how long professional oven cleaning takes</a> breaks this down. Some tenants book a dedicated <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">oven clean</a> alongside the main clean so the rest of the work can carry on in parallel.</p>
<h3>Bathrooms and limescale</h3>
<p>Hard water across much of East Yorkshire means limescale builds up quickly on taps, screens and toilets. Descaling products need time to work, and thick scale may need more than one application.</p>
<h3>Kitchen cupboards and appliances</h3>
<p>Every cupboard is emptied and wiped inside and out, along with the fridge freezer, washing machine, dishwasher and microwave. In a large kitchen with lots of units, this adds up.</p>
<h3>Detail work</h3>
<p>Skirting boards, door frames, radiators, switches and windowsills are quick individually, but there are a lot of them, especially in Victorian and Edwardian terraces with tall skirting, picture rails and sash windows.</p>
<h2>What makes an end of tenancy clean take longer?</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Condition:</strong> a property cleaned regularly during the tenancy is far quicker than one that's been left to build up.</li>
<li><strong>Belongings still in place:</strong> cleaners can't clean properly around boxes and furniture.</li>
<li><strong>Pets:</strong> hair works its way into carpets, upholstery and skirting boards.</li>
<li><strong>Mould and condensation:</strong> common in older terraces and slow to treat properly.</li>
<li><strong>Extra areas:</strong> conservatories, garages, utility rooms, balconies and second bathrooms.</li>
<li><strong>Carpet and upholstery cleaning:</strong> adds time on site and drying time afterwards.</li>
<li><strong>Access and parking:</strong> upper-floor flats without a lift, or streets with limited parking, slow down getting equipment in and out.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same factors shape the price of a clean, which we explain in <a href="{{guide:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-cost-factors}}">what affects the cost of end of tenancy cleaning</a>.</p>
<h2>How long does it take to do an end of tenancy clean yourself?</h2>
<p>Expect a DIY clean to take much longer than a professional one. You're likely to be working alone or in a pair, with household products rather than professional equipment, and often while tired from packing and moving.</p>
<p>The biggest time-saver is working methodically. Follow an <a href="{{guide:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-checklist}}">end of tenancy cleaning checklist</a> room by room, top to bottom, so you never have to go back over a room you've already finished.</p>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Put the oven racks and grill pan to soak in a large plastic tub or heavy-duty bin bag with degreaser before you start anything else. By the time you reach the kitchen, most of the grease will wipe off.</p></div>
<h2>Planning your move-out cleaning timeline</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Two to three weeks before:</strong> read the check-in inventory, book any professional cleaning, and arrange rubbish and furniture removal.</li>
<li><strong>The week before:</strong> run down the fridge and freezer, wipe cupboards as you pack them, and start tackling limescale.</li>
<li><strong>Moving day:</strong> get everything out, including from the loft, shed and yard.</li>
<li><strong>The day after you move out:</strong> do the full clean, or have it done, in the empty property.</li>
<li><strong>Before check-out:</strong> allow drying time, do a final walk-round and take dated photos.</li>
</ol>
<p>Carpets cleaned by hot water extraction often dry within a few hours with good ventilation, though thick carpets and cold, damp weather can mean longer. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:how-long-carpets-take-to-dry}}">how long carpets take to dry after cleaning</a> explains what affects it. For a schedule covering the whole move, see our <a href="{{guide:moving-house-cleaning-timeline}}">week-by-week moving house cleaning timeline</a>.</p>
<h2>How to help a professional clean go faster</h2>
<ul>
<li>Have the property completely empty, including cupboards, wardrobes and the fridge</li>
<li>Make sure the electricity and water are on and every room has working lights</li>
<li>Arrange keys and any alarm codes in advance, or use our key holding</li>
<li>Tell us about parking, stairs, pets and any problem areas when you book</li>
<li>Share the check-in inventory if you have it, so we know the standard expected</li>
</ul>
<p>Our guide to <a href="{{guide:preparing-for-your-first-clean}}">preparing for a professional clean</a> has more practical tips.</p>
<h2>Booking an end of tenancy clean that fits your move</h2>
<p>We're open 7 days a week, which makes it easier to fit a clean between your move-out and your check-out appointment. Our team covers <a href="{{local:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-hull}}">end of tenancy cleaning in Hull</a> and towns across the East Riding, including <a href="{{area:driffield}}">Driffield</a> and <a href="{{area:goole}}">Goole</a>, working to letting-agent checklists with a fixed quote agreed up front.</p>
<p>Find out what's included in our <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy cleaning service</a>, or <a href="{{page:contact}}">tell us about your property</a> and we'll give you a realistic idea of how long it will take.</p>
