		'content'     => <<<'HTML'
<p>Student house move-out cleaning in Hull means returning the whole property, not just your own room, to the condition recorded on the check-in inventory. In a shared house, the kitchen, bathrooms, hallway and living room are everyone's responsibility, and on a joint tenancy any deductions usually come out of the group's deposit.</p>
<p>From terraces off Newland Avenue to houses near Cottingham Road and Newland Park, student lets around the University of Hull tend to have the same jobs missed at check-out. This guide covers how to split the work fairly, a shared-house checklist, rubbish and bin day, and how to protect your deposit.</p>
<div class="cc-takeaways"><p class="cc-takeaways__title">Key takeaways</p><ul><li>Shared areas are everyone’s responsibility, so agree a cleaning plan as a house.</li><li>On a joint tenancy, one person’s mess can lead to deductions from everyone’s deposit.</li><li>Arrange bulky waste collections and plan around bin day well before you leave.</li><li>Take dated photos of every room, including shared spaces, before you hand back the keys.</li></ul></div>
<h2>Check your tenancy end date and check-out arrangements</h2>
<p>Student tenancies don't always end when exams do. Many run through the summer, and agents often have lots of check-outs to fit in around the same time. Before you plan anything:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check the end date on your tenancy agreement and whether you can leave earlier</li>
<li>Find the check-in inventory, and ask the agent for a copy if nobody kept one</li>
<li>Ask how keys should be returned and whether you can attend the check-out</li>
<li>Find out whether the agent has its own move-out cleaning checklist</li>
</ul>
<p>If housemates are leaving on different dates, agree who will be last out. That person shouldn't be left to clean the whole house on their own.</p>
<h2>Joint tenancies: why the whole house shares the risk</h2>
<p>Most student houses are let on a joint tenancy, with everyone named on one agreement. The deposit is often protected as a single sum, so a deduction for a greasy kitchen or a stained hallway carpet can come out of the group's money, even if your own room is spotless.</p>
<p>Agree in writing, even in the group chat, how you'll split the cleaning and any deductions before anyone leaves. Our guide on <a href="{{guide:how-to-get-your-deposit-back}}">how to get your deposit back in full</a> explains how deposit protection and deductions work.</p>
<p>Under the Tenant Fees Act 2019, landlords and agents can't charge you a fee for professional cleaning, but they can deduct the reasonable cost of cleaning if the house is left dirtier than it was at check-in. If you'd rather share the cost of a professional <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy clean</a> between you, that's your choice.</p>
<h2>How to split student house cleaning fairly</h2>
<p>Each person cleans their own bedroom. Shared areas are where things go wrong, so divide them up and set a deadline that leaves time for checking. A simple rota for a five-bed house might look like this:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Area</th><th>Who</th><th>Key jobs</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Kitchen: oven, hob and extractor</td><td>Housemate 1</td><td>Degrease the oven, racks and door glass; clean the hob and filters</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kitchen: cupboards, fridge and floor</td><td>Housemate 2</td><td>Empty and wipe cupboards, defrost and clean the fridge freezer, mop</td></tr>
<tr><td>Bathroom and toilets</td><td>Housemate 3</td><td>Descale taps and shower, clean the toilet, scrub grout and sealant</td></tr>
<tr><td>Living room, hall and stairs</td><td>Housemate 4</td><td>Dust skirting and frames, hoover the stairs, wipe switches and doors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Rubbish, bins and yard</td><td>Housemate 5</td><td>Book bulky waste, clear the yard, empty and rinse the bins</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>The oven is usually the hardest job, so give it to someone with time or split it between two people. Everyone should do a final walk-round together before the keys go back.</p>
<h2>Student house move-out cleaning checklist</h2>
<p>Use this alongside our full <a href="{{guide:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-checklist}}">room-by-room end of tenancy cleaning checklist</a>.</p>
<h3>Your bedroom</h3>
<ul>
<li>Remove everything, including from under the bed and on top of the wardrobe</li>
<li>Take down posters and remove Blu Tack carefully</li>
<li>Wipe the desk, drawers, wardrobe inside and out, windowsill and skirting boards</li>
<li>Hoover the carpet and mattress, and check both for stains</li>
</ul>
<h3>The kitchen</h3>
<ul>
<li>Clear every cupboard, the fridge and the freezer, including food nobody claims</li>
<li>Degrease the oven, hob, extractor and splashback</li>
<li>Wipe cupboards inside and out, the worktops, sink and taps</li>
<li>Clean the microwave, the washing machine drawer and the door seal</li>
<li>Mop the floor, including behind the bin and along the kickboards</li>
</ul>
<p>A shared student oven can be a serious job. Splitting the cost of a specialist <a href="{{service:oven-cleaning}}">oven clean</a> between the house is often the easiest answer.</p>
<h3>Bathrooms</h3>
<ul>
<li>Descale taps, shower heads and screens, as Hull's hard water leaves scale quickly</li>
<li>Clear hair from plugholes and clean the toilet inside and out</li>
<li>Treat black mould on grout and silicone</li>
<li>Clean the extractor fan, mirror and floor</li>
</ul>
<h3>Living room, hallway and stairs</h3>
<ul>
<li>Dust skirting boards, door frames, radiators and light fittings</li>
<li>Wipe marks from walls, doors and light switches</li>
<li>Hoover the stairs and hallway carpet, which take the most wear</li>
<li>Clear coats, shoes and old post from the hallway</li>
</ul>
<p>Older terraced houses near the university are prone to condensation. If there's black mould on window frames or bedroom walls, our guide to <a href="{{guide:damp-and-mould-in-older-homes}}">condensation and black mould in older Hull homes</a> explains how to deal with it. If it's a recurring problem, make sure it's been reported to the landlord in writing.</p>
<h2>Rubbish, unwanted furniture and bin day</h2>
<p>Rubbish left behind is one of the most avoidable deductions. Agents can charge for clearing bags, broken furniture or old mattresses from the house and yard.</p>
<ul>
<li>Check your bin day and plan the final clear-out around it, so bins aren't left overflowing</li>
<li>Look up bulky waste collections and household waste recycling centres on the Hull City Council website well in advance, or East Riding of Yorkshire Council if your house is in <a href="{{area:cottingham}}">Cottingham</a></li>
<li>Donate usable items rather than leaving them in the yard</li>
<li>Never leave bags or furniture in the back alley: it counts as fly-tipping and can lead to a fine</li>
</ul>
<div class="cc-callout"><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Agree a cut-off date for food and belongings a couple of days before the clean. Anything still in the house after that is donated or binned.</p></div>
<h2>What your landlord is responsible for in a student HMO</h2>
<p>Many student houses are houses in multiple occupation (HMOs). Larger HMOs, with five or more people forming two or more households, need a mandatory licence, and councils can add other licensing schemes, so landlords should check with Hull City Council or East Riding of Yorkshire Council. GOV.UK explains <a href="https://www.gov.uk/house-in-multiple-occupation-licence" rel="noopener">HMO licensing</a> in more detail.</p>
<p>HMO management regulations require the manager to keep common parts, such as hallways and stairs, clean and in good repair during the tenancy. That doesn't remove your responsibility to leave the house as you found it, but it does mean landlords should be looking after shared areas too. Our guide to <a href="{{guide:hmo-cleaning-responsibilities}}">HMO cleaning responsibilities</a> explains the split, and some landlords book regular <a href="{{service:communal-area-cleaning}}">communal area cleaning</a> for this reason.</p>
<h2>Protecting your deposit when you leave</h2>
<ol>
<li>Clean once the house is empty, not while people are still packing.</li>
<li>Walk round together with the check-in inventory.</li>
<li>Take dated photos and video of every room, including shared areas, the oven and inside the fridge.</li>
<li>Save the photos somewhere everyone can access, such as a shared folder.</li>
<li>Return every key, including window and bike shed keys, and get written confirmation.</li>
<li>Give the agent forwarding addresses for everyone.</li>
</ol>
<p>Knowing <a href="{{guide:what-letting-agents-check-at-check-out}}">what letting agents check at check-out</a> helps you focus on the right areas. If you disagree with any deductions, the deposit scheme's free dispute service can decide, and our guide to <a href="{{guide:deposit-dispute-cleaning-evidence}}">deposit disputes over cleaning</a> explains how to present your evidence.</p>
<h2>Help with student house cleaning in Hull</h2>
<p>If exams, travel or a summer job leave no time to clean, our team can do the whole house in one visit before your check-out, following the agent's inventory checklist. We clean student lets across <a href="{{local:end-of-tenancy-cleaning-hull}}">Hull</a> and Cottingham, with a fixed quote agreed up front that housemates can split, and a re-clean guarantee if something isn't right.</p>
<p>Find out more about our <a href="{{service:end-of-tenancy-cleaning}}">end of tenancy cleaning</a>, or <a href="{{page:contact}}">get in touch</a> with your address and move-out date.</p>
